        <h2 class="section-title-underline mb-5">
          <span>Why Academics Works</span>
        </h2>
        <p>
          E-Learning, or electronic learning, began in the 1960s with early computer-based training systems designed for military and corporate use.
          By the 1990s, the growth of the internet made education more flexible and accessible, allowing online courses to reach a global audience.
          In the 2000s, universities adopted Learning Management Systems (LMS) such as Moodle and Blackboard, enabling structured online programs.
          The 2010s saw the rise of Massive Open Online Courses (MOOCs) on platforms like Coursera, edX, and Udemy, bringing quality education to millions worldwide.
          Today, E-Learning continues to evolve through AI, mobile learning, and virtual classrooms, shaping the future of digital education.
        </p>
